WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.359
Joaquin Phoenix may play a man with an uncontrollable
cackle in director Todd Phillips' Joker, but

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.560
he certainly wasn't laughing in an interview
about the dark and gritty comic book movie.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.210
Phoenix, who stars as failed comedian Arthur
Fleck in the Joker origin story film releasing

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.960
on October 4, sat down for an interview with
The Telegraph's Robbie Collin.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.860
During the conversation, Collin asked Phoenix
if he was worried that his performance might

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.060
inspire real world copycat killers.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.820
The question apparently took Phoenix by surprise,
causing the actor to briefly walk out of the

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:29.320
interview.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.880
Collin described what happened:

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:36.280
"Unlike Heath Ledger's inscrutable take on
the character in 2008's The Dark Knight, Phoenix's

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.410
Arthur Fleck, a failed comedian who still
lives with his elderly mother, is the horribly

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:41.820
familiar enemy within.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.320
If the film hadn't been set in the '80s he
could easily be the latest online message-board

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.740
extremist to take his grievances murderously
viral.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.160
Yet Phoenix doesn't seem to have considered
this kind of question at all.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.280
So when I put it to him aren't you worried
that this film might perversely end up inspiring

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.840
exactly the kind of people it's about, with
potentially tragic results?

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.760
his fight-or-flight response kicks in."

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.560
Phoenix reportedly replied,

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:04.520
"Why?

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.120
Why would you?

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.380
No… no."

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.240
According to Collin, Phoenix then stood up,
shook his hand, and left the interview.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.880
"all I have are negative thoughts."

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.220
Roughly an hour later largely spent talking
with Warner Bros.' PR team Phoenix returned

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.300
to finish the interview.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.000
The actor explained that he left because he
had never considered the possibility, and

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.640
needed time to try and figure out an appropriate
response.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.840
But the interview concluded without Phoenix
answering the question.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.660
And it's a question that many critics are
already asking.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:37.520
Though most reviewers gave Joker high marks
after its worldwide debut at the Venice International

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:43.080
Film Festival on August 31, 2019, more than
one also suggested that the film may send

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:48.200
a dangerous message and give viewers the wrong
idea about avenging their own personal grievances.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.900
Vanity Fair critic Richard Lawson wondered
in his review of Joker whether the film, quote,

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.680
"may be irresponsible propaganda for the very
men it pathologizes."

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.180
Stephanie Zacharek at Time felt that Arthur
Fleck comes across uncomfortably similar to

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:05.160
some of the most violent, dangerous people
who have inflicted incredible harm to people,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.480
particularly in America.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:07.760
She wrote in part,

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.180
"All of Arthur's travails are leading up to
a series of 'See what you made me do?'

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.240
brutalities, most of which happen while he's
dressed up in his clown suit.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.340
Violence makes him feel more in control, less
pathetic.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.000
Killing [...] empowers him [...] The movie
lionizes and glamorizes Arthur even as it

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.100
shakes its head, faux-sorrowfully, over his
violent behavior."

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.440
Collin himself tweeted after watching Joker
at the Venice Film Festival that he thinks

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.660
the movie is good, but he's, quote, "worried
someone's going to get killed" because of

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:35.160
it.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.799
He stated in his full review, published by
The Telegraph,

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.540
"This is a film that is going to stir up trouble
in the consciences of everyone who watches

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.859
it, and almost certainly in the outside world
as well."

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.800
"When I was a little boy and told people I
was going to be a comedian, everyone laughed

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:51.300
at me.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.420
Well, no one's laughing now!"

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.680
Other critics, though, have defended the film.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:00.380
The Outline's Alex Nichols argued that the
idea of Joker's violence potentially resulting

00:03:00.389 --> 00:03:04.980
in real-life tragedies, quote, "rests entirely
on the assumption that violent media breeds

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:05.900
violent behavior."

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.980
Stephen Galloway of The Hollywood Reporter
agreed, arguing that the only people who would

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.160
be inspired to violence are people predisposed
to it already.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:14.020
He said there's

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.359
"[no] evidence to show that a criminal even
one who imitates something on film wouldn't

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.080
have done something equally abominable at
another time."

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:25.700
It seems this type of complexity and ambiguity
may have been one of the biggest reasons why

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.040
Phoenix signed on for Joker in the first place.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.290
As the actor told Collin after coming back
for the interview, he liked that Arthur Fleck

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:35.820
as a character, quote, "pos[ed] questions
with no easy answers."

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.980
Decide how you feel about Joker when the film
opens in theaters on October 4.

